    46  /**********************************************************************/
    47  void *pj_malloc(size_t size) {
    48  /***********************************************************************
    49  Currently, pj_malloc is a hack to solve an errno problem.
    50  The problem is described in more details at
    51  https://bugzilla.redhat.com/bugzilla/show_bug.cgi?id=86420.
    52  It seems, that pj_init and similar functions incorrectly
    53  (under debian/glibs-2.3.2) assume that pj_malloc resets
    54  errno after success. pj_malloc tries to mimic this.
    55
    56  NOTE (2017-09-29): The problem described at the bugzilla page
    57  referred to above, is most likely a case of someone not
    58  understanding the proper usage of errno. We should review
    59  whether "the problem is actually a problem" in PROJ.4 code.
    60
    61  Library specific allocators can be useful, and improve
    62  interoperability, if properly used. That is, by making them
    63  run/initialization time switchable, somewhat like the file i/o
    64  interface.
    65
    66  But as things stand, we are more likely to get benefit
    67  from reviewing the code for proper errno usage, which is hard,
    68  due to the presence of context local and global pj_errnos.
    69
    70  Probably, these were introduced in order to support incomplete
    71  implementations of thread local errnos at an early phase of the
    72  implementation of multithreading support in PROJ.4).
    73
    74  It is likely too late to get rid of contexts, but we can still
    75  benefit from a better usage of errno.
    76  ***********************************************************************/
    77      int old_errno = errno;
    78      void *res = malloc(size);
    79      if ( res && !old_errno )
    80              errno = 0;
    81      return res;
    82  }
